{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795427486,"nonce":"+tsl3B/VDgZUE8BftXv7iGHiLtk=","title":"The interpreter refused a native of more than 12 words and passed stack arguments wrongly from 9","message":"natives.rs called a native through a ladder of all-i64 signatures that ended at 12 words, and refused anything wider with \"Native call with N args not yet supported\" (hxn-gpu-vulkan's createGraphicsPipeline, 14 arguments plus the receiver, in --mode interp and hybrid).\n\nPast the integer registers the callee reads its later arguments from the stack, laid out as the platform says. Apple arm64 packs a narrow argument into the bytes it needs; the ladder passed every argument as a full 8-byte word. So 9 to 12 word natives returned wrong answers without any error: a 12-argument native whose arguments are 1..12 weighted by position returned 395, not 650, on macOS arm64. A native mixing booleans among stack integers is wrong in the same way. Compiled code was right in every case (--mode jit), because Cranelift lowers the call. Floats had a separate dispatcher generated through 8 arguments.\n\nFix: a call of more than 8 words goes through a buffer-in thunk that Cranelift builds from the native's signature (codegen.rs compile_native_thunk, sharing the emitter with compile_uniform_entry) and the interpreter caches per callee (wide_native.rs). Any arity, integers, booleans, f32, f64 and float results, every platform. The hand-written 9 to 12 word cases are removed; calls of 8 words or fewer are unchanged.\n\nEvidence: crates/ash_cli/tests/wide_native.rs, with natives of 9, 12 and 14 integer arguments, one mixing booleans, one interleaving int, f32 and f64, and one returning a float from a stack argument; interp, hybrid and jit print the same lines. Passes on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 (NUC). CI job: aot executable, which runs on both Linux architectures and macOS. Windows not tested; the test is Unix only.","files":null}]}